{"conclusion_raw":"","conclusion_rendered":"","difficulty":"Easy","documents":[],"flags":[{"title":"Featured Student Guide","flagid":"GUIDE_FEATURED_STUDENT","text":"This guide has been the hard work of our awesome students and is found to be exceptionally cool by the iFixit staff."}],"guideid":182837,"image":{"id":3571313,"guid":"tvQyWErVxU4tdLNn","mini":"https:\/\/guide-images.cdn.ifixit.com\/igi\/tvQyWErVxU4tdLNn.mini","thumbnail":"https:\/\/guide-images.cdn.ifixit.com\/igi\/tvQyWErVxU4tdLNn.thumbnail","140x105":"https:\/\/guide-images.cdn.ifixit.com\/igi\/tvQyWErVxU4tdLNn.140x105","200x150":"https:\/\/guide-images.cdn.ifixit.com\/igi\/tvQyWErVxU4tdLNn.200x150","standard":"https:\/\/guide-images.cdn.ifixit.com\/igi\/tvQyWErVxU4tdLNn.standard","440x330":"https:\/\/guide-images.cdn.ifixit.com\/igi\/tvQyWErVxU4tdLNn.440x330","medium":"https:\/\/guide-images.cdn.ifixit.com\/igi\/tvQyWErVxU4tdLNn.medium","large":"https:\/\/guide-images.cdn.ifixit.com\/igi\/tvQyWErVxU4tdLNn.large","huge":"https:\/\/guide-images.cdn.ifixit.com\/igi\/tvQyWErVxU4tdLNn.huge","original":"https:\/\/guide-images.cdn.ifixit.com\/igi\/tvQyWErVxU4tdLNn.full"},"introduction_raw":"If your sink is clogged and you're looking for a quick way to clear it, this guide will walk you through using a wet\/dry vacuum to effectively unclog your sink without the need for harsh chemicals or professional assistance.\n\nThis method is suitable for most standard sinks. Keep in mind that some stopper plugs are attached to the p-trap and may require extra precautions when removing them. Follow instructions [link|https:\/\/www.youtube.com\/watch?v=Wj7VZVcoU48&t=7s|here] to learn how to do that. Additionally, some sinks have an overflow hole that should be sealed during this process to maintain proper suction.\n\nBefore beginning, make sure your sink is free of standing water and that your vacuum is ready for wet use. If needed, check your vacuum\u2019s settings to ensure it's set up for liquid suction.","introduction_rendered":"<p>If your sink is clogged and you're looking for a quick way to clear it, this guide will walk you through using a wet\/dry vacuum to effectively unclog your sink without the need for harsh chemicals or professional assistance.<\/p>\n\n<p>This method is suitable for most standard sinks. Keep in mind that some stopper plugs are attached to the p-trap and may require extra precautions when removing them. Follow instructions <a href=\"https:\/\/www.youtube.com\/watch?v=Wj7VZVcoU48&amp;t=7s\">here<\/a> to learn how to do that. Additionally, some sinks have an overflow hole that should be sealed during this process to maintain proper suction.<\/p>\n\n<p>Before beginning, make sure your sink is free of standing water and that your vacuum is ready for wet use. If needed, check your vacuum\u2019s settings to ensure it's set up for liquid suction.<\/p>","featured_document_embed_url":"","featured_document_thumbnail_url":"","locale":"en","parts":[],"prerequisites":[],"steps":[{"title":"","lines":[{"text_raw":"Before starting, make sure your sink is empty.","bullet":"icon_note","level":0,"lineid":null,"text_rendered":"Before starting, make sure your sink is empty."},{"text_raw":"Clear out the sink using a cup or sponge to remove any standing water for better suction.","bullet":"black","level":0,"lineid":null,"text_rendered":"Clear out the sink using a cup or sponge to remove any standing water for better suction."}],"guideid":182837,"stepid":390348,"orderby":1,"revisionid":3641733,"pending_patrol":{"gsr":null,"gst":null},"media":{"type":"image","data":[{"id":3571313,"guid":"tvQyWErVxU4tdLNn","mini":"https:\/\/guide-images.cdn.ifixit.com\/igi\/tvQyWErVxU4tdLNn.mini","thumbnail":"https:\/\/guide-images.cdn.ifixit.com\/igi\/tvQyWErVxU4tdLNn.thumbnail","140x105":"https:\/\/guide-images.cdn.ifixit.com\/igi\/tvQyWErVxU4tdLNn.140x105","200x150":"https:\/\/guide-images.cdn.ifixit.com\/igi\/tvQyWErVxU4tdLNn.200x150","standard":"https:\/\/guide-images.cdn.ifixit.com\/igi\/tvQyWErVxU4tdLNn.standard","440x330":"https:\/\/guide-images.cdn.ifixit.com\/igi\/tvQyWErVxU4tdLNn.440x330","medium":"https:\/\/guide-images.cdn.ifixit.com\/igi\/tvQyWErVxU4tdLNn.medium","large":"https:\/\/guide-images.cdn.ifixit.com\/igi\/tvQyWErVxU4tdLNn.large","huge":"https:\/\/guide-images.cdn.ifixit.com\/igi\/tvQyWErVxU4tdLNn.huge","original":"https:\/\/guide-images.cdn.ifixit.com\/igi\/tvQyWErVxU4tdLNn.full"}]},"comments":[]},{"title":"","lines":[{"text_raw":"If your stopper is attached to the p-trap, removing it may cause water to leak. Be ready to duct tape it or have a towel or small bucket to catch the leak.","bullet":"icon_caution","level":0,"lineid":null,"text_rendered":"If your stopper is attached to the p-trap, removing it may cause water to leak. Be ready to duct tape it or have a towel or small bucket to catch the leak."},{"text_raw":"If your sink has a stopper plug, take it out.","bullet":"black","level":0,"lineid":null,"text_rendered":"If your sink has a stopper plug, take it out."},{"text_raw":"This will give direct access to the clogged drain and improve suction.","bullet":"icon_note","level":1,"lineid":null,"text_rendered":"This will give direct access to the clogged drain and improve suction."},{"text_raw":"Most sinks have detached stopper plugs. However, some sink stoppers are connected to the p-trap, so check which type you have and adjust accordingly.","bullet":"icon_note","level":0,"lineid":null,"text_rendered":"Most sinks have detached stopper plugs. However, some sink stoppers are connected to the p-trap, so check which type you have and adjust accordingly."}],"guideid":182837,"stepid":390353,"orderby":2,"revisionid":3641733,"pending_patrol":{"gsr":null,"gst":null},"media":{"type":"image","data":[{"id":3571305,"guid":"eFYsWSmNwTkwUPcu","mini":"https:\/\/guide-images.cdn.ifixit.com\/igi\/eFYsWSmNwTkwUPcu.mini","thumbnail":"https:\/\/guide-images.cdn.ifixit.com\/igi\/eFYsWSmNwTkwUPcu.thumbnail","140x105":"https:\/\/guide-images.cdn.ifixit.co